      <!-- Section 5: Engineering Trade-offs & Limitations -->
      <section class="section">
        <div class="section-head">
          <h2>5. Decision Trade-offs &amp; Analytical Governance</h2>
          <p>Pragmatic evaluation rationale and critical data contract boundaries.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid two-col">
          <article class="card">
            <h3 style="margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 1.05rem;">Operational Decision Rationale</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.55;">
              <code>Frequency Business</code> was selected as the simpler candidate for operational evaluation due to its intuitive ranking structure, transparency for business stakeholders, and zero model maintenance or inference latency overhead.
              On this holdout, its average Precision@K was slightly higher than Logistic Regression (+0.54% at K=20%), but the confidence interval did not establish superiority.
              This is a pragmatic operational choice based on available evidence, not a conclusion that heuristic rules always outperform machine learning.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3 style="margin: 0 0 8px; font-size: 1.05rem;">Data &amp; Label Boundaries</h3>
            <p style="margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.55;">
              The target label captures <strong>natural repurchase events observed in historical data within the 90-day window</strong> following the prediction cutoff.
              Because the dataset does not record active marketing interventions, randomized controlled trials (RCTs), or intervention costs,
              <strong>these metrics do not represent marketing campaign response, causal uplift, or business return on investment (ROI)</strong>.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

